  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 113 PM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nA few showers will push into the Coastal Bend this afternoon, before \ndissipating inland. Moisture will increase tonight in combination of \na mid-level shortwave, causing PWAT values to near 2.0\" (99th \npercentile) and bringing scattered to numerous showers over the \nCoastal Bend early Tuesday morning. Coverage will shift inland along \nthe seabreeze in the afternoon, followed by a lull in activity \nTuesday night. We won't have as good of mid-level support for \nconvection on Wednesday, but there remains a weak inverted surface \ntrough to aid in convergence along with the seabreeze and enhanced \nmoisture to allow for a medium chance of showers and low chance for \nthunderstorms over the Coastal Plains.\n\nRemnants of what is currently Tropical Storm Vance over the east \nPacific will move into Mexico and advect even more moisture over \nSouth Texas Thursday, more focused along the Rio Grande. Therefore, \nmedium to high rain chances (40-70%) will shift over the Brush \nCountry and Rio Grande Plains on Thursday, with lower chances \neastward. Rain chances lower to 20-45% Friday as the remnants move \nnortheastward across the state. Total rainfall through Friday \nremains around an inch or less areawide, with locally higher amounts \nup to around 2\".\n\nHeading into Halloween weekend, we'll maintain low rain chances due \nto abundant moisture and diurnal processes. The mid-level flow \nbecomes more zonal with a subtle shortwave remaining off to our \nnorth, not aiding in lift. There are signs of our next cold front \npushing through early next week Monday night into Tuesday. We'll \nneed to keep a close eye on this progression.\n\nLastly, minor coastal flooding will likely develop during high tide \neach afternoon into evening for the Middle Texas Coast through the \nwork week, as swell periods remain high along with a persistent \neasterly flow and astronomical high tide. In addition, these \nconditions will increase the risk of rip currents, creating more \ndangerous swimming conditions.",
